# rules: use-of-sha1
# languages: kotlin
# fixture: kotlin/CWE-327-sha1.kt
# from: kotlin/lang/security/use-of-sha1.yaml
#
# SHA-1 collisions have been real since SHAttered in 2017 and cheap since 2020,
# and a chosen-prefix collision is what breaks a signature: two documents, both
# meaningful, one digest.
#
# As translated the rule asked for `$VAR = $MD.getInstance("$ALGO")`, an
# assignment, which Kotlin almost never writes - a value arrives through `val`
# or `var`, which is a declaration and a different node. Naming the call and
# nothing else finds it wherever it stands. See use-of-md5, which had the same
# hole for the same reason.
#
# SHA-1 is still correct as an HMAC key derivation step and inside PBKDF2, and
# it is required outright by protocols that are not going to change - the
# WebSocket handshake computes one, and a certificate pin in the older format
# is one. This rule does not know the difference and cannot: the judgement is
# the reader's, which is the honest place for it to land. Over LeakCanary the
# one finding is a heap-dump identity hash, which is a checksum and is fine.#
# The helpers are written with their argument, for the reason use-of-md5 gives:
# okio's `ByteString.sha1()` takes none, and over OkHttp a pattern without the
# arity reported a certificate pin and the WebSocket accept key - both real
# SHA-1, both required by the protocol they implement, neither a weakness. The
# argument-less spelling is deliberately not matched.

["$MD.getInstance($ALGO)"] as $made
| ["$DU.getSha1Digest().digest($$$_)", "DigestUtils.sha1($X)",
   "DigestUtils.sha1Hex($X)", "$DU.sha1Hex($X)"] as $helpers
| scan_ast("*.kt"; $made + $helpers) as $all
| ( ($all | of($made) | where_capture("ALGO"; "^\"(?:SHA1|SHA-1|sha1|sha-1)\"$"))
    + ($all | of($helpers)) )
| finding("use-of-sha1";
    "SHA-1 collisions have been cheap since 2020, so two inputs with this "
    + "digest can be produced to order; SHA-256 instead")
| report
